WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.388
A B+tree rewrites pages in place, so random inserts mean scattered disk writes.

2
00:00:08.388 --> 00:00:12.996
A log-structured merge tree, or LSM tree, makes them sequential.

3
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.021
A write goes to two places.

4
00:00:15.021 --> 00:00:22.643
It is appended to a log on disk, so a crash can't lose it, and it goes into the memtable, a sorted map in memory.

5
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:24.669
Two more arrive, 3 before 2.

6
00:00:24.669 --> 00:00:29.109
The memtable keeps them in key order, and no page on disk is touched.

7
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:38.973
When the memtable is full, it is flushed to disk in key order, in one sequential write: an SSTable, a sorted string table, which never changes.

8
00:00:38.973 --> 00:00:41.400
The log and the memtable start over.

9
00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:46.455
Then auth:2 is captured, so a newer version goes into the memtable.

10
00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:51.204
A delete of auth:3 writes a tombstone, a marker that says deleted.

11
00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:53.742
The next flush makes a second table.

12
00:00:53.742 --> 00:00:57.911
Now two keys have two versions on disk, and the newer table wins.

13
00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:04.599
A read checks the memtable, then the tables from newest to oldest, and stops at the first hit.

14
00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:07.213
get(auth:2) finds CAPTURED in table 2.

15
00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:11.899
get(auth:1) is not in table 2, but checking costs a disk read.

16
00:01:11.899 --> 00:01:16.940
So every table has a Bloom filter: a few bits per key, kept in memory.

17
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:25.109
The filter of table 2 says definitely not here, so the read skips that table and finds auth:1 in table 1.

18
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:29.496
get(auth:3) stops at the tombstone in table 2: not found.

19
00:01:29.496 --> 00:01:32.856
Reading on would bring the deleted value back.

20
00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:39.068
Compaction merges tables, like merge sort, and keeps only the newest version of each key.

21
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:45.055
The tombstone is dropped as well, since no older table is left that could hold auth:3.

22
00:01:45.055 --> 00:01:47.932
Now a read checks one table instead of two.

23
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.183
So every write is a cheap append.

24
00:01:50.183 --> 00:01:55.400
A read may look in several tables, and Bloom filters and compaction keep that cheap.
